title ALES52 — Lotus's envelope follower: the dynamics time base opens
prompt take the next agent-doable Lindy-first crux-first lap, self-approve the design round, send each finished increment to origin and xykj61, stop only at the custody gates in REMEMBER
think With the limiter (ALES49), compressor (ALES50), and gate (ALES51) all instantaneous, the Lindy-first crux is the one thing all three want underneath them — a time base. Crux-first: build the envelope follower ONCE and prove it, so the attack/release versions of the limiter, compressor, and gate all read the same proven follower rather than each re-deriving a smoother. It is the hardest-solvable move that opens the rest.
think Design: follow(clip, start, count, attack_num, attack_den, release_num, release_den) in place, replacing each sample with the running envelope magnitude. target = min(|x|, sample_max) (a full-scale negative peak reads at the rail so the envelope fits i16); gap = target - env; step by the attack fraction when rising, the release fraction when falling; a forced unit step when the fraction truncates to zero yet the gap remains — reusing ALES40's exact-reach guarantee and ALES43's carried state (a single i64 env carried by the caller).
think Crux chosen: unit attack AND unit release (both 1:1) make step = gap, so env = min(|x|, sample_max) every sample — the peak follower with no smoothing IS the rectified signal, byte-for-byte by construction (the envelope's counterpart to the compressor's unit-ratio identity); and the carried state makes a span followed in two pieces equal the whole once, which is precisely what the coming attack/release dynamics need to span and automate.
obs Caught one test-authorship red BEFORE claiming green: clause 3 first asserted exact reach of a 1:4 attack over only 8 samples — the geometric step decays ~(3/4)^n and needs ~30 steps before the forced unit step finishes, so 8 was far too few. Fixed by lengthening the constant-input buffers to 64 samples (Zig `** 64` / `++`); the exact-reach law is a finite-steps guarantee, not an every-length one. The code was correct; the test was impatient.
obs GREEN across seven clauses; ALES51 gate re-run stays GREEN, so the time base rests beside the inverted floor. TAME bans clean; lotus/envelope.rye carries plenty of asserts (not on the zero-assert ratchet). Second rung of this turn (ALES51 gate · ALES52 follower), each sent as its own signed round to origin and xykj61.
loom witness=ales_envelope_witness green=1 clauses=7 family=dynamics rung=ALES52 reuses=timeline,tone,gate
file lotus/envelope.rye the envelope follower — render the running magnitude, attack rising and release falling, unit coefficients the instantaneous magnitude, forced unit step exact-reach, carried state
file tools/ales_envelope_witness.rish builds, runs the selftest, asserts GREEN ales-envelope, re-proves ALES51 gate green
file active-designing/20260814-172539_fill-ales52-lotus-envelope-follower.md the self-approved design round for ALES52
file lotus/README.md front door synced — the ALES52 envelope-follower sentence and the status header
recommend keep-going the dynamics family now holds the brickwall, the softened ratio, the inverted floor, and their shared time base; the next crux is to wire the envelope into the limiter, compressor, or gate so each leans in over an attack and eases off over a release — a time-based gate reading the follower is the cleanest first
